# Which data files have had their procedures written up, and which have
# not yet.
#
# doc/CONTRIBUTING.md asks every procedure in the interpreter's own
# PostScript for a header: the stack effect on its definition line, and
# prose above saying which dictionary is current when it runs, which VM
# bank its definitions land in, whether it runs once as the file loads
# or waits for a caller, who calls it, and what a change would trip.
# Most of these files were written before that was asked of them, so the
# work is being done a file at a time and this says how far it has got.
#
# The register exists so the check can enforce forwards rather than wait
# for the last file. tests/check-proc-spec.sh holds an annotated file to
# the rule and leaves a pending one alone, so a file written up today
# cannot quietly lose an effect tomorrow, and the pending lines are a
# work list rather than a standing excuse. A pending file that has come
# to satisfy the rule is reported by the check, not accepted, because a
# line that has stopped being true is the line nobody rereads.
#
# What annotated means is only what a line scan can see: every procedure
# the scan recognises states a stack effect, in the dot form, once. The
# prose is the part that carries the orientation and no check reads it,
# so annotated is a floor and review is what puts a file over it.
#
#   file <name>  annotated  its procedures state their effects
#   file <name>  pending    some do not, yet
#
